1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,188
The page will not load. Where do you even start?

2
00:00:03,738 --> 00:00:08,303
Most people refresh, restart, and hope. A good engineer does something different.

3
00:00:08,853 --> 00:00:10,441
You test one layer at a time,

4
00:00:10,441 --> 00:00:13,781
and the first test that fails tells you where the problem is.

5
00:00:14,331 --> 00:00:16,315
Today you learn six tools for that,

6
00:00:16,315 --> 00:00:17,562
and you use them live,

7
00:00:17,562 --> 00:00:19,942
on a small network built on this computer.

8
00:00:21,992 --> 00:00:25,192
Here is the diagnosis ladder. One question per rung.

9
00:00:25,742 --> 00:00:30,734
Rung one: is this machine set up? The ip command shows your address, and your way out.

10
00:00:31,284 --> 00:00:33,307
Rung two: does the name work?

11
00:00:33,307 --> 00:00:34,353
Dig asks D N S.

12
00:00:34,353 --> 00:00:37,492
N s lookup is an older tool for the same job.

13
00:00:38,042 --> 00:00:42,714
Rung three: can I reach the machine? Ping sends a message, and waits for the reply.

14
00:00:43,264 --> 00:00:47,659
Rung four: where does the path stop? Trace route lists every router on the way.

15
00:00:48,209 --> 00:00:51,040
Rung five: is anything listening on the port?

16
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:52,550
S s shows the listeners,

17
00:00:52,550 --> 00:00:54,438
and curl tries the connection.

18
00:00:54,988 --> 00:00:58,615
The first rung that fails is your suspect. You do not need to guess.

19
00:01:00,665 --> 00:01:04,505
First, know your own setup. Type: ip -br addr

20
00:01:04,635 --> 00:01:06,522
Each line is an interface.

21
00:01:06,522 --> 00:01:07,175
This one,

22
00:01:07,175 --> 00:01:07,683
c zero,

23
00:01:07,683 --> 00:01:08,118
is up,

24
00:01:08,118 --> 00:01:11,457
with the address ten dot zero dot one dot two,

25
00:01:11,457 --> 00:01:12,763
slash twenty-four.

26
00:01:13,313 --> 00:01:15,681
Now the way out. Type: ip route

27
00:01:15,811 --> 00:01:18,238
The first line is the default route.

28
00:01:18,238 --> 00:01:22,889
Anything not on my own network goes via ten dot zero dot one dot one.

29
00:01:22,889 --> 00:01:24,238
That is the gateway.

30
00:01:24,788 --> 00:01:29,481
No address, or no default route? Stop here. Nothing above this rung will work.

31
00:01:31,531 --> 00:01:32,896
Now reach out.

32
00:01:32,896 --> 00:01:35,529
Ping sends a small message,

33
00:01:35,529 --> 00:01:37,675
and waits for a reply.

34
00:01:37,675 --> 00:01:38,260
First,

35
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:39,430
the gateway.

36
00:01:39,430 --> 00:01:41,771
Type: ping -c 3 10.0.1.1

37
00:01:41,901 --> 00:01:42,622
Three sent,

38
00:01:42,622 --> 00:01:43,540
three replies.

39
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:45,310
The time is the round trip,

40
00:01:45,310 --> 00:01:46,359
in milliseconds.

41
00:01:46,359 --> 00:01:47,408
It is tiny here,

42
00:01:47,408 --> 00:01:49,965
because everything runs on one machine.

43
00:01:50,515 --> 00:01:55,592
Now the server. Type: ping -c 3 10.0.2.2

44
00:01:55,722 --> 00:02:01,056
Zero percent packet loss. Loss is the number to watch. Any loss means a flaky link.

45
00:02:01,606 --> 00:02:09,648
Now an address with no route. Type: ping -c 2 -W 1 10.0.3.9

46
00:02:09,778 --> 00:02:11,553
Destination net unreachable,

47
00:02:11,553 --> 00:02:13,075
reported by the gateway.

48
00:02:13,075 --> 00:02:16,434
The router is saying: I do not know how to get there.

49
00:02:17,984 --> 00:02:25,685
Ping tells you if you can reach it. Trace route shows how. Type: traceroute -n 10.0.2.2

50
00:02:25,815 --> 00:02:27,222
Each line is one hop,

51
00:02:27,222 --> 00:02:28,763
one router on the path.

52
00:02:28,763 --> 00:02:31,242
The dash n option skips name lookups,

53
00:02:31,242 --> 00:02:32,983
so the output stays clean.

54
00:02:33,533 --> 00:02:35,211
Hop one is the gateway,

55
00:02:35,211 --> 00:02:37,326
ten dot zero dot one dot one.

56
00:02:37,326 --> 00:02:39,441
Hop two is the server itself.

57
00:02:39,441 --> 00:02:40,098
Two hops,

58
00:02:40,098 --> 00:02:41,192
and we arrived.

59
00:02:41,742 --> 00:02:42,620
On a long path,

60
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:44,201
a hop where the time jumps,

61
00:02:44,201 --> 00:02:45,665
or where the stars begin,

62
00:02:45,665 --> 00:02:47,481
shows where the trouble starts.

63
00:02:48,031 --> 00:02:53,620
It works by sending packets with a tiny time to live, so each router in turn reports back.

64
00:02:55,670 --> 00:03:02,497
Now the server side. This terminal is on the server. Is anything listening? Type: ss -ltn

65
00:03:02,627 --> 00:03:03,763
L is listening,

66
00:03:03,763 --> 00:03:04,595
T is T C P,

67
00:03:04,595 --> 00:03:06,110
and N shows numbers.

68
00:03:06,110 --> 00:03:08,609
One listener: port eighty eighty,

69
00:03:08,609 --> 00:03:11,032
on ten dot zero dot two dot two.

70
00:03:11,582 --> 00:03:18,259
What about port 9090? Type: ss -ltn sport = :9090

71
00:03:18,389 --> 00:03:19,916
Only the header comes back.

72
00:03:19,916 --> 00:03:21,838
Nothing is listening on that port,

73
00:03:21,838 --> 00:03:24,213
so a client that tries it will be refused.

74
00:03:26,263 --> 00:03:28,181
Now a real incident.

75
00:03:28,181 --> 00:03:30,195
A page will not load,

76
00:03:30,195 --> 00:03:32,401
so we climb the ladder.

77
00:03:32,401 --> 00:03:32,977
First,

78
00:03:32,977 --> 00:03:34,799
a typo in the name.

79
00:03:34,799 --> 00:03:37,484
Type: ping -c 1 wbe.lab.test

80
00:03:37,614 --> 00:03:42,734
Name or service not known. The name rung failed, so stop there, and fix the spelling.

81
00:03:43,284 --> 00:03:48,874
With the right name, ask D N S. Type: dig +short web.lab.test

82
00:03:49,004 --> 00:03:53,804
An address comes back: ten dot zero dot two dot two. The name rung is fine.

83
00:03:54,354 --> 00:03:59,431
Can we reach it? Type: ping -c 1 10.0.2.2

84
00:03:59,561 --> 00:04:02,270
One reply. The address and the path are fine.

85
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:13,594
Now the page, on port 9090. Type: curl -sS -m 3 http://web.lab.test:9090

86
00:04:13,724 --> 00:04:14,843
Failed to connect.

87
00:04:14,843 --> 00:04:16,334
Everything below worked,

88
00:04:16,334 --> 00:04:18,013
so the problem is the port.

89
00:04:18,013 --> 00:04:19,318
Nothing is listening,

90
00:04:19,318 --> 00:04:20,934
just as the server showed.

91
00:04:21,484 --> 00:04:29,506
Try the right port. Type: curl -s http://web.lab.test:8080

92
00:04:29,636 --> 00:04:33,305
Hello from the lab. Fixed in five commands, with no guessing.

93
00:04:35,355 --> 00:04:36,891
Match the message to the rung.

94
00:04:37,441 --> 00:04:42,156
Name or service not known: D N S. Check the spelling, and the resolver.

95
00:04:42,706 --> 00:04:46,716
Network is unreachable: I P. Check your address, and your gateway.

96
00:04:47,266 --> 00:04:52,600
Destination net unreachable: a router has no route. Trace route shows which one.

97
00:04:53,150 --> 00:04:57,630
No answer at all, or only stars: a firewall, or a machine that is down.

98
00:04:58,180 --> 00:05:02,297
Connection refused: the machine answers, but nothing listens on that port.

99
00:05:02,847 --> 00:05:06,922
Connection timed out: often a firewall quietly dropping the packets.

100
00:05:08,972 --> 00:05:10,401
Watch out for four myths.

101
00:05:10,951 --> 00:05:16,071
Myth one: if ping fails, the server is down. Many servers block ping on purpose.

102
00:05:16,621 --> 00:05:18,054
Myth two: if ping works,

103
00:05:18,054 --> 00:05:19,129
the website works.

104
00:05:19,129 --> 00:05:21,337
Ping only proves the machine answers,

105
00:05:21,337 --> 00:05:23,427
not that the web server is running.

106
00:05:23,977 --> 00:05:27,284
Myth three: stars in trace route mean a broken router.

107
00:05:27,284 --> 00:05:30,163
Many routers just do not reply to those probes.

108
00:05:30,713 --> 00:05:35,727
Myth four: restart first, investigate later. Restarting can erase the evidence.

109
00:05:36,277 --> 00:05:37,791
Test one layer at a time.

110
00:05:39,841 --> 00:05:42,166
Here is how you check your own understanding.

111
00:05:42,716 --> 00:05:45,063
Explain the ladder, and what each rung checks.

112
00:05:45,613 --> 00:05:49,602
Read ip addr and ip route: address, network, and gateway.

113
00:05:50,152 --> 00:05:53,416
Run and read a ping: replies, time, and packet loss.

114
00:05:53,966 --> 00:05:57,017
Run and read a trace route: the hops, and where it stops.

115
00:05:57,567 --> 00:06:01,428
Use dig, curl, and s s to check a name, a port, and a listener.

116
00:06:01,978 --> 00:06:03,856
Match an error message to its rung.

117
00:06:04,406 --> 00:06:06,027
To pass, you need all six.

118
00:06:08,077 --> 00:06:10,360
Your turn, in the virtual machine you built.

119
00:06:10,910 --> 00:06:14,387
One: run ip -br addr, and find your address.

120
00:06:14,937 --> 00:06:18,158
Two: run ip route, and find your default gateway.

121
00:06:18,708 --> 00:06:22,740
Three: ping your gateway, then ping a public site, and compare the times.

122
00:06:23,290 --> 00:06:25,699
Four: run trace route to a public site,

123
00:06:25,699 --> 00:06:26,872
and count the hops.

124
00:06:26,872 --> 00:06:27,922
If it is missing,

125
00:06:27,922 --> 00:06:29,157
install it with apt.

126
00:06:29,707 --> 00:06:34,272
Five: run s s dash l t n, and list what is listening on your machine.

127
00:06:34,822 --> 00:06:39,494
Six: break something on purpose. Ping a made-up name, and say which rung failed.

128
00:06:40,044 --> 00:06:43,351
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

129
00:06:58,901 --> 00:06:59,776
Quick check.

130
00:07:00,326 --> 00:07:02,459
Which tool lists every hop on the path?

131
00:07:03,009 --> 00:07:04,929
What does connection refused tell you?

132
00:07:05,479 --> 00:07:06,951
What does packet loss mean?

133
00:07:07,501 --> 00:07:09,357
Which command shows what is listening?

134
00:07:09,907 --> 00:07:14,110
Objective covered: MC03-O05.

135
00:07:14,660 --> 00:07:16,729
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

136
00:07:27,279 --> 00:07:29,711
Next: routing, N A T, and firewalls.

137
00:07:30,261 --> 00:07:32,138
Subscribe, so you do not miss it.
